ser - the serial ports, and a terminal on one

  ser                          every port, and what each is set to
  ser <port>                   a terminal on it, at whatever it is set to
  ser <port> 115200 8N1        set the line, then a terminal
  ser <port> set 9600 7E1      set the line and stop
  ser <port> break             hold the line at break for a moment

A serial line tells you nothing about itself. Both ends have to have
been told the same rate and the same character, and when they have not
the result is not silence but rubbish on the screen, so the listing
says what every port is set to and the terminal says what it opened
with before the first byte arrives.

A line is written the way it has always been written: the rate, then
the bits, the parity and the stop bits. `8N1` is eight bits, no parity
and one stop bit, which is what everything that is not a modem uses;
`7E1` is the other one anybody meets. Parity is one of N, O, E, M or S,
for none, odd, even, mark and space.

A port comes from whatever found it. A USB serial adapter appears here
as soon as it is plugged in and disappears when it is pulled out, and
`usb` shows the same device from the bus's side. The id column is the
maker and the model, which is how two adapters of different kinds are
told apart.

The state column is what the far end says its own end of the line is
doing, as the names on a connector: DCD that something is there, DSR
that it is ready, RI that it is ringing. Many adapters have nowhere to
say any of this and the column reads `says nothing`, which is not a
fault. `break`, `framing`, `parity` and `overrun` are faults, and the
last three mean the two ends disagree about the line.

The terminal sends what is typed, a character at a time, and shows what
comes back. Nothing is echoed here: whether what was typed appears is
the far end's decision, and a device that shows nothing is usually one
that is not listening rather than one that is not there. Return is sent
as a carriage return and backspace as a delete, which is what a serial
console expects. F10 leaves, and it is a plain key rather than a chord
because every chord worth having is a byte the far end wants.

One program to a port, and the last to ask has it: opening a port that
somebody else had leaves their end closed rather than splitting the
traffic between two readers, neither of which would then get the whole
of it. A program that stopped without giving a port back does not hold
it forever.

`[bytes lost]` in the middle of a terminal means something arrived with
nowhere to put it. It is said out of band because the one place it
cannot be said is among the bytes that had nowhere to go.
